---
// src/pages/lab/frame-diff-wgsl.astro
// STC-320 — Lab study two: the frame-difference kernel ported to WGSL /
// WebGPU. Fully client-side; no API, no KV. Unlisted and noindex, matching
// study one (/lab/frame-diff) — neither is in labEntries yet, because
// neither has the real footage STC-319's rules list needs.
//
// The cross-check table below is live: it runs the fixtures through every
// implementation the visitor's browser can provide and reports what agreed.
// It is deliberately on the page rather than only in the test suite —
// "the same fixture produces the same change grid in both studies" is this
// ticket's own done-condition, and a claim a reader can watch execute is
// worth more than one they have to take on trust.
export const prerender = true;

import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Frame difference — WGSL"
  description="Porting a WebGL2 frame-difference kernel to WGSL and WebGPU, and turning the change grid from a raster into a compute-shader reduction."
  ogTitle="Frame difference, WGSL — lab study"
  noindex={true}
>
  <section class="fdw container">
    <p class="fdw__overline">Study two &bull; WGSL frame difference</p>
    <h1 class="fdw__heading">The same kernel, in WGSL.</h1>
    <p class="fdw__intro">
      <a href="/lab/frame-diff">Study one</a> wrote a frame-difference kernel in GLSL: two video
      frames in, a mask of what changed out, plus one number per frame for the plot. The recorder's
      compositor is locked to WGSL, so this is the version that ships &mdash; and porting code you
      wrote a week ago is a good way to find out what you actually decided, as opposed to what the
      language decided for you.
    </p>
    <p class="fdw__intro">
      One thing came back. Study one's per-frame number was never the fraction of the frame that
      changed; it was a <strong>point sample of a fixed 64&times;36 lattice</strong>, which on a
      640&times;360 clip reads one pixel in a hundred. A text caret is two pixels wide. Below,
      measured rather than argued: study one's estimator sees a blinking caret in
      <strong>2 of the 10 positions</strong> it can occupy, and which two is a property of the
      lattice rather than of the recording.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fdw__h2">Cross-check</h2>
    <p class="fdw__body">
      Every implementation this browser can run, over the same fixtures. The rule itself lives in
      one place (<code>frame-diff-rule.ts</code>, plain TypeScript, no GPU) and both shaders are
      ports of <em>that</em> rather than of each other &mdash; so when two disagree, the one that
      disagrees with the reference is the one that is wrong. Fixtures marked with an expected value
      are correct by construction, which is what stops three ports of one mistake certifying each
      other.
    </p>
    <p class="fdw__body fdw__body--tight">
      <strong>There are two reference columns because there are two rules.</strong> Study one point
      samples and study two reduces, so each shader is held to the model it actually implements:
      GLSL against <em>point sample</em>, WGSL against <em>reduction</em>. Those two columns
      differing is the finding, not a fault &mdash; compare each shader with the reference column
      directly to its left.
    </p>

    <div class="fdw__impls" id="fdw-impls"></div>
    <div class="fdw__table-wrap">
      <table class="fdw__table" id="fdw-table">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">
          Change-rule cross-check: each fixture's changed-pixel fraction under every available
          implementation.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Fixture</th>
            <th scope="col">Expected</th>
            <th scope="col">Reference<br /><span class="fdw__th-sub">reduction</span></th>
            <th scope="col">Reference<br /><span class="fdw__th-sub">point sample</span></th>
            <th scope="col">GLSL<br /><span class="fdw__th-sub">study one</span></th>
            <th scope="col">WGSL<br /><span class="fdw__th-sub">study two</span></th>
            <th scope="col">Result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="fdw-tbody">
          <tr><td colspan="7">Running…</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="fdw__verdict" id="fdw-verdict" aria-live="polite"></p>

    <h2 class="fdw__h2">The caret sweep</h2>
    <p class="fdw__body">
      A 2&times;16 caret blinks on against a light field, at each of the ten horizontal positions
      that cover one period of the sampling lattice. The reduction counts the same 32 pixels every
      time. The estimator's answer depends on where the caret landed.
    </p>
    <p class="fdw__sweep" id="fdw-sweep">Running…</p>
    <p class="fdw__body fdw__body--tight">
      This matters more than a factor-of-six error bar would suggest, because a caret is not an
      edge case &mdash; it is study one's own example of the change a viewer <em>should</em> be
      looking at, and the case that motivates keying auto-zoom off frame difference instead of the
      cursor in the first place. An estimator that misses it 80% of the time is not a noisy version
      of the right answer; on the signal that matters most it is a coin toss.
    </p>

    <h2 class="fdw__h2">What changed in the port</h2>
    <ol class="fdw__notes">
      <li>
        <strong>The grid became a reduction.</strong> Study one gets its number by rendering the
        diff into a 64&times;36 framebuffer and reading it back &mdash; with nearest filtering,
        each output texel is one source pixel, so the result is an estimator, not a total. A
        compute shader has no such constraint: one workgroup per grid cell, every pixel of that
        cell's tile visited exactly once, an atomic counter per cell. The two therefore produce
        <em>different numbers by design</em>, and the table above holds each to the model it
        actually implements rather than pretending they should match.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>textureLoad</code>, not <code>textureSample</code>.</strong> A sampler brings
        filtering and wrap modes with it, and a reduction wants exact texel fetches. Dropping the
        sampler also drops study one's <code>UNPACK_FLIP_Y_WEBGL</code> from the compute path
        entirely &mdash; the flip survives only in the eye-view pass, which really is a raster.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Acquisition is asynchronous, so the constructor had to go.</strong>
        <code>getContext('webgl2')</code> returns a context or null on the spot;
        <code>requestAdapter</code> and <code>requestDevice</code> are promises. The class is built
        through a static <code>create()</code>, and readback is <code>mapAsync</code> against study
        one's synchronous <code>readPixels</code>. Two API shape changes, both forced by the
        platform rather than chosen.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Errors arrive on a side channel.</strong> A GLSL compile failure is one synchronous
        call away at the point of the mistake. WGSL validation surfaces through
        <code>popErrorScope</code>, asynchronously, and an unhandled one is a blank canvas that
        looks like a rendering bug. <code>create()</code> wraps module creation in an error scope
        and refuses rather than returning a half-built renderer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Float width is part of the rule, which the port is what surfaced.</strong> The
        reference was written in float64 and the shaders run in float32. A fixture engineered to
        sit exactly on the threshold lands 5.6e&minus;17 <em>below</em> it in float64 and exactly
        <em>on</em> it in float32, because the Rec.601 weights sum to 0.99999999999999989 rather
        than 1 &mdash; so the reference disagreed with both shaders over arithmetic rather than
        over the rule. The reference rounds to float32 now. Exact-boundary behaviour is a
        documented convention between the three ports, not a checked one: no portable fixture can
        pin it.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="fdw__h2">Live footage</h2>
    <p class="fdw__body">
      The same take study one plays, through the WGSL kernel. It needs both WebGPU and an H.264
      decoder, and says which one is missing rather than showing an empty canvas.
    </p>
    <div class="fdw__frame">
      <canvas
        class="fdw__canvas"
        id="fdw-canvas"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Frame-difference mask computed in WGSL: the current frame dimmed, with pixels that changed since the previous frame highlighted in red."
      ></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="fdw__controls">
      <button type="button" id="fdw-play" class="pill-button pill-button--outlined" aria-pressed="false" disabled>
        Play
      </button>
      <button type="button" id="fdw-step" class="pill-button pill-button--outlined" disabled>Step &rarr;</button>
      <label class="fdw__threshold">
        Threshold
        <input type="range" id="fdw-threshold" min="0.01" max="0.5" step="0.01" value="0.08" />
        <span id="fdw-threshold-value">0.08</span>
      </label>
      <span class="fdw__frame-index" id="fdw-frame-index">not loaded</span>
    </div>
    <p class="fdw__note" id="fdw-live-note"></p>

    <h2 class="fdw__h2">What this study does not settle</h2>
    <p class="fdw__body">
      The port is verified against the reference wherever a browser could run it; the
      implementation panel above says which legs actually executed here, and a leg that did not run
      is never counted as agreeing. What none of it establishes is
      <strong>which change is the change</strong> &mdash; the temporal rule separating a caret from
      a spinner from a live chart tick. That is
      <a href="/lab/frame-diff">study one's</a> deliverable, it needs 30&ndash;60 seconds of real
      footage that does not exist yet, and it remains unwritten. What this study adds to it is a
      correction worth having first: on the signal that rule is most about, the measurement study
      one was going to be read off was structurally unable to see it.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .fdw {
    padding-top: var(--space-2xl);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .fdw__overline {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-body-small);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-on-surface-muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .fdw__heading {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--text-display-large);
    line-height: var(--lh-display-large);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .fdw__h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--text-headline-medium);
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .fdw__intro,
  .fdw__body {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-body-large);
    line-height: var(--lh-body-large);
    color: var(--color-on-surface-dimmed);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
  }

  .fdw__body--tight {
    font-size: var(--text-body-medium);
  }

  .fdw__impls {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .fdw__impl {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-body-small);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.75rem;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  .fdw__impl--ok {
    border-color: currentColor;
  }

  .fdw__impl--off {
    color: var(--color-on-surface-muted);
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  .fdw__table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .fdw__table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-body-small);
  }

  .fdw__table th,
  .fdw__table td {
    text-align: right;
    padding: 0.45rem 0.6rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .fdw__table th:first-child,
  .fdw__table td:first-child {
    text-align: left;
  }

  .fdw__th-sub {
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--color-on-surface-muted);
  }

  .fdw__cell--off {
    color: var(--color-on-surface-muted);
  }

  .fdw__cell--bad {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .fdw__cell--detail {
    text-align: left;
    white-space: normal;
    color: var(--color-on-surface-dimmed);
    background: var(--color-surface-subtle);
  }

  .fdw__verdict,
  .fdw__sweep,
  .fdw__note {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-body-medium);
    line-height: var(--lh-body-large);
    color: var(--color-on-surface-dimmed);
    background: var(--color-surface-subtle);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
  }

  .fdw__notes {
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-body-large);
    line-height: var(--lh-body-large);
    color: var(--color-on-surface-dimmed);
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .fdw__notes li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .fdw__frame {
    height: clamp(240px, 45vh, 420px);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    overflow: clip;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    background: var(--color-surface-subtle);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .fdw__canvas {
    display: block;
    max-width: 100%;
    max-height: 100%;
    object-fit: contain;
  }

  .fdw__controls {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .fdw__threshold,
  .fdw__frame-index {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-body-small);
    color: var(--color-on-surface-dimmed);
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .fdw__table {
      font-size: 0.75rem;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { runCrossCheck, type CrossCheckReport } from '../lib/frame-diff-crosscheck.ts';
  import { FrameDiffGpuRenderer, checkGpuSupport } from '../lib/frame-diff-gpu.ts';
  import { loadSession, type LoadedSession } from '../lib/session/session.ts';
  import { decodeAll } from '../lib/session/decode.ts';
  import type { Anchors, SessionEvent } from '../lib/session/types.ts';

  // Study one's assets. Deliberately the same take, so "the same fixture
  // produces the same change grid in both studies" is about one recording.
  const ASSET_BASE = '/lab/study/frame-diff';

  function fmt(v: number | null): string {
    if (v === null) return '—';
    if (v === 0) return '0';
    if (v < 1e-4) return v.toExponential(2);
    return v.toFixed(6);
  }

  function initFrameDiffWgsl() {
    const tableMaybe = document.getElementById('fdw-tbody');
    if (!tableMaybe || tableMaybe.dataset.fdwInit === 'true') return;
    tableMaybe.dataset.fdwInit = 'true';
    // Bound to a non-null const before any closure captures it: TypeScript
    // cannot keep a narrowing across the async callbacks below, and the
    // alternative is a `!` at every use site.
    const table: HTMLElement = tableMaybe;

    const implsEl = document.getElementById('fdw-impls')!;
    const verdictEl = document.getElementById('fdw-verdict')!;
    const sweepEl = document.getElementById('fdw-sweep')!;
    const liveNoteEl = document.getElementById('fdw-live-note')!;
    const canvas = document.getElementById('fdw-canvas') as HTMLCanvasElement;
    const playBtn = document.getElementById('fdw-play') as HTMLButtonElement;
    const stepBtn = document.getElementById('fdw-step') as HTMLButtonElement;
    const thresholdInput = document.getElementById('fdw-threshold') as HTMLInputElement;
    const thresholdValue = document.getElementById('fdw-threshold-value')!;
    const frameIndexEl = document.getElementById('fdw-frame-index')!;

    // ---- Cross-check -----------------------------------------------------
    async function crossCheck() {
      let report: CrossCheckReport;
      try {
        report = await runCrossCheck();
      } catch (e) {
        table.innerHTML = '';
        const tr = document.createElement('tr');
        const td = document.createElement('td');
        td.colSpan = 7;
        td.textContent = `Cross-check failed: ${e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e)}`;
        tr.appendChild(td);
        table.appendChild(tr);
        return;
      }

      // The REASON an implementation could not run goes on the page, not in a
      // title attribute. A tooltip is unreachable on a touch device and
      // invisible to anyone reading rather than hovering — and this reason is
      // the single most useful sentence on the page when a column is empty.
      implsEl.innerHTML = '';
      for (const impl of report.implementations) {
        const span = document.createElement('span');
        span.className = `fdw__impl ${impl.available ? 'fdw__impl--ok' : 'fdw__impl--off'}`;
        span.textContent = impl.available
          ? `\u2713 ${impl.label}`
          : `\u2717 ${impl.label}${impl.reason ? ` \u2014 ${impl.reason}` : ''}`;
        implsEl.appendChild(span);
      }

      table.innerHTML = '';
      for (const row of report.rows) {
        const tr = document.createElement('tr');
        const failed = row.disagreements.length > 0;
        const cells: Array<{ text: string; off?: boolean; title?: string; bad?: boolean }> = [
          { text: row.fixture, title: row.purpose },
          { text: row.expected === null ? '\u2014' : fmt(row.expected), off: row.expected === null },
          { text: fmt(row.oracle) },
          { text: fmt(row.oraclePointSample) },
          { text: fmt(row.glsl), off: row.glsl === null },
          { text: fmt(row.wgsl), off: row.wgsl === null },
          {
            // Names the LEG, so a reader can tell "the port is wrong" from
            // "the reference is wrong" from "this browser could not run it".
            text: failed
              ? row.disagreements.map((d) => d.leg).join(' + ')
              : row.glsl === null && row.wgsl === null
                ? 'reference only'
                : 'ok',
            bad: failed,
          },
        ];
        for (const c of cells) {
          const td = document.createElement('td');
          td.textContent = c.text;
          if (c.off) td.className = 'fdw__cell--off';
          if (c.bad) td.className = 'fdw__cell--bad';
          if (c.title) td.title = c.title;
          tr.appendChild(td);
        }
        table.appendChild(tr);

        // A failing row gets its numbers spelled out underneath, because
        // "MISMATCH" with no figure is the report this page gave on its first
        // run against real hardware and it cost a round trip to diagnose.
        if (failed) {
          for (const d of row.disagreements) {
            const detailRow = document.createElement('tr');
            const td = document.createElement('td');
            td.colSpan = 7;
            td.className = 'fdw__cell--detail';
            td.textContent = `${row.fixture} \u2014 ${d.detail}`;
            detailRow.appendChild(td);
            table.appendChild(detailRow);
          }
        }
      }

      // The verdict names what did NOT run. A cross-check reporting
      // "everything agreed" while two of three legs never executed is the
      // "success by finding nothing to do" failure this project keeps
      // meeting, and it is not going to be introduced here on purpose.
      const ran = report.implementations.filter((i) => i.available).map((i) => i.label);
      const missing = report.implementations.filter((i) => !i.available);
      const parts: string[] = [];
      if (report.agree) {
        parts.push('Every implementation that ran agreed.');
      } else {
        const legs = [...new Set(report.rows.flatMap((r) => r.disagreements.map((d) => d.leg)))];
        parts.push(
          `MISMATCH in: ${legs.join(', ')}` +
            (report.caretSweep.reductionStable ? '' : ', caret-sweep stability') +
            '. Each failing row is spelled out under the table.',
        );
      }
      parts.push(`Ran: ${ran.join(', ')}.`);
      if (missing.length > 0) {
        parts.push(
          `Did not run here: ${missing.map((m) => `${m.label} (${m.reason})`).join('; ')} — ` +
            'those columns are unverified in this browser, not passing.',
        );
      }
      verdictEl.textContent = parts.join(' ');

      const s = report.caretSweep;
      sweepEl.textContent =
        `Over ${s.positions} positions: study one's point-sampled estimator registered the caret ` +
        `in ${s.pointSampleSaw}; study two's reduction counted ${s.reductionPixels} changed pixels ` +
        `at ${s.reductionStable ? 'every one' : 'SOME BUT NOT ALL — the reduction is sampling'}.`;

      (window as any).__frameDiffWgslTest = { report };
    }

    // ---- Live footage ----------------------------------------------------
    let renderer: FrameDiffGpuRenderer | null = null;
    let bitmaps: ImageBitmap[] = [];
    let session: LoadedSession | null = null;
    let index = 1;
    let running = false;
    let rafId = 0;
    let lastStep = 0;
    const FPS_MS = 1000 / 12;

    function threshold() {
      return Number(thresholdInput.value);
    }

    function drawCurrent() {
      if (!renderer || bitmaps.length < 2) return;
      renderer.draw(bitmaps[index - 1]!, bitmaps[index]!, threshold());
      frameIndexEl.textContent = `frame ${index} / ${bitmaps.length - 1}`;
    }

    function tick(t: number) {
      rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      if (!running) return;
      if (t - lastStep >= FPS_MS) {
        lastStep = t;
        index = index >= bitmaps.length - 1 ? 1 : index + 1;
        drawCurrent();
      }
    }

    async function bootLive() {
      const support = await checkGpuSupport();
      if (!support.supported) {
        liveNoteEl.textContent = `Live view unavailable: ${support.reason} The cross-check above still ran everything else this browser supports.`;
        return;
      }
      try {
        liveNoteEl.textContent = 'Loading take…';
        const [anchors, eventsDoc, displayMp4] = await Promise.all([
          fetch(`${ASSET_BASE}/anchors.json`).then((r) => r.json() as Promise<Anchors>),
          fetch(`${ASSET_BASE}/events.json`).then(
            (r) => r.json() as Promise<{ version: number; events: SessionEvent[] }>,
          ),
          fetch(`${ASSET_BASE}/display.mp4`).then((r) => r.arrayBuffer()),
        ]);
        session = await loadSession({ anchors, events: eventsDoc, displayMp4 });
        liveNoteEl.textContent = 'Decoding frames…';
        bitmaps = await decodeAll(session.video);
        renderer = await FrameDiffGpuRenderer.create(canvas);
        index = Math.min(1, bitmaps.length - 1);
        drawCurrent();
        playBtn.disabled = false;
        stepBtn.disabled = false;
        liveNoteEl.textContent = `Loaded ${bitmaps.length} frames through the WGSL kernel.`;
      } catch (e) {
        // The overwhelmingly likely failure is H.264 decoding, which several
        // browsers (and every headless Linux CI runner this project has met)
        // simply do not have. Say which step failed rather than "failed".
        liveNoteEl.textContent =
          `Live view unavailable: ${e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e)}. ` +
          'This is usually a missing H.264 decoder rather than a WebGPU problem — the cross-check above is unaffected, since it builds its frames in-page.';
      }
    }

    playBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      running = !running;
      lastStep = 0;
      playBtn.textContent = running ? 'Pause' : 'Play';
      playBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(running));
    });
    stepBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      running = false;
      playBtn.textContent = 'Play';
      playBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'false');
      if (bitmaps.length >= 2) {
        index = index >= bitmaps.length - 1 ? 1 : index + 1;
        drawCurrent();
      }
    });
    thresholdInput.addEventListener('input', () => {
      thresholdValue.textContent = threshold().toFixed(2);
      drawCurrent();
    });

    void crossCheck();
    void bootLive();
    rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);

    document.addEventListener(
      'astro:before-swap',
      () => {
        cancelAnimationFrame(rafId);
        renderer?.dispose();
        for (const b of bitmaps) b.close();
        delete (window as any).__frameDiffWgslTest;
      },
      { once: true },
    );
  }

  initFrameDiffWgsl();
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', initFrameDiffWgsl);
</script>
